reference files derive from skills/networking/dns/powerdns/. MIT licence governs all files.

Files folded -> reference mapping:
  skills/networking/dns/powerdns/SKILL.md
    -> SKILL.md (umbrella body: product overview, when-to-use/not-use, reference router,
       core task snippets for Auth/Recursor/DNSdist, cross-references, red flags, bottom line)
    -> references/architecture.md (suite overview, Auth internal architecture, all backend
       details gmysql/gpgsql/gsqlite3/gldap/bind/pipe/remote, DNSSEC implementation with full
       pdnsutil commands, REST API endpoint table, Lua hooks table, views, zone transfer and
       supermaster/autoprimary, Recursor internal architecture, YAML config, DNSSEC validation
       modes, RPZ triggers and actions, serve-stale, Recursor Lua, DNSdist load-balancing
       policies, DoH/DoT/DoQ frontend, rules engine selectors and actions, health checks,
       DNSdist Defender, deployment patterns, pdns.conf core settings reference)
  skills/networking/dns/powerdns/references/architecture.md
    -> references/architecture.md (merged with SKILL.md detail above; source architecture.md
       contributed backend architecture diagrams, Recursor MTasker diagram, YAML configuration
       sections, RPZ implementation details, DNSdist rules engine tables, deployment patterns,
       and TSIG zone-transfer configuration)
    -> references/diagnostics.md (created from the upstream "Common Pitfalls" section and
       the pdnsutil/pdns_control/DNSdist operational detail scattered through both upstream
       files; expanded with rec_control commands, DNSdist console commands, Prometheus metrics,
       Carbon/Graphite export, common failure patterns with diagnosis/resolution steps, and log
       reference tables; none of this was a standalone section in the upstream; the split
       provides progressive disclosure between configuration depth and operational runbook)

Customisations applied:
  - Re-authored the agent-style upstream framing ("You are a specialist in...", "How to
    Approach Tasks") into vault operator-facing prose: four-field frontmatter, skill marker
    block, product-classification-first orientation, When-to-use / When-not-use, reference
    router table, cross-references, red flags, bottom line.
  - References carry no frontmatter and no skill marker (line 1 is the # Title in each).
  - Split single upstream architecture.md (269 lines, architecture + ops mixed) into two
    focused references: architecture.md (configuration depth and deployment patterns) and
    diagnostics.md (commands, metrics, failure patterns, log reference); the split is
    justified by the volume and the distinct audience: architecture is read when building or
    configuring; diagnostics is read during incidents.
  - Upstream used ASCII `--` in some diagram labels and em-dash-style comments in config
    blocks. All converted to inline comments, parenthetical pauses, or ASCII `->` arrows.
  - British/Pacific English applied throughout: authorised, licence (noun), behaviour,
    analyse, organisation, synchronise, optimise, recognise.
  - Upstream ASCII art diagrams converted from box-drawing characters (which may not render
    in all terminals) to plain `+---+` style for portability; flow arrows use `->`.
  - Cross-references added to vault skills: dns-network-ops (architecture/concepts boundary),
    multi-vendor-network-ops (change-control contract), secrets-hygiene (API key and DNSSEC
    private key material), postgres-best-practices (gpgsql), mysql-best-practices (gmysql),
    utc-timestamps (SOA serials, DNSSEC key timing), systematic-debugging, oncall-runbooks.
  - TSIG zone-transfer configuration and supermaster/autoprimary detail added to
    architecture.md; these appeared only as a "pitfall" in the upstream and are expanded
    to operational guidance.
  - DNSdist Defender section expanded with specific attack-type names (PRSD, DNS tunneling)
    to match the upstream SKILL.md detail that was not in architecture.md.
  - Diagnostics reference is entirely new structure: no single upstream section maps to it;
    content drawn from the upstream "Common Pitfalls" list and operational commands scattered
    through both upstream files.
  - Em-dash (U+2014) and en-dash (U+2013) glyphs: zero occurrences across all files.
    ASCII "->" used for arrows; commas, semicolons, and parentheses for dash-style pauses.

Boundary notes:
  powerdns-ops = PowerDNS IMPLEMENTATION (Auth Server, Recursor, DNSdist configuration,
  operations, backends, DNSSEC operations with pdnsutil, REST API, Lua scripting, metrics).
  dns-network-ops owns DNS ARCHITECTURE + CROSS-PLATFORM (resolution concepts, DNSSEC
  chain-of-trust design, zone-transfer protocol concepts, platform selection, cross-platform
  comparison, hybrid design patterns). No content duplication: dns-network-ops covers the
  conceptual and comparative layer; powerdns-ops covers the PowerDNS-specific implementation.

